#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302270 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=-2t+\\frac{3}{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | $21$ | $56$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | {{field-3}} | $-\\frac{13}{2}$ | $-\\frac{17}{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x={t}^{3}-2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=(2)^{3}-2(2)=8-4=4.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=-2t+\\frac{3}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=-2(3)+\\frac{3}{2}=-6+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{12}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(5),$ we substitute $t=5$ into $x={t}^{3}-2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(5)=(5)^{3}-2(5)=125-10=115.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $21$ | $56$ | $115$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{13}{2}$ | $-\\frac{17}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302271 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-2t+2,\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12,\\,-1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $5$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $12$ | $11$ | {{field-2}} | $39$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into $x={t}^{2}-2t+2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(1)=(1)^{2}-2(1)+2=1-2+2=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+12=16-12+12=16.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(4)=2(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}+12=128-48+12=92.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $12$ | $11$ | $16$ | $39$ | $92$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302272 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}-2,\\,y=\\frac{t-1}{t+1},\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{23}{2}$ | $30$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(5),$ we substitute $t=5$ into $x=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(5)=\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{3}-2=\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{4}{2}=\\frac{121}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=\\frac{t-1}{t+1}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=\\frac{2-1}{2+1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(5),$ we substitute $t=5$ into $y=\\frac{t-1}{t+1}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(0)=\\frac{5-1}{5+1}=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{23}{2}$ | $30$ | $\\frac{121}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302273 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,},\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t,\\,-3\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-1}} | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{33}{2}$ | $8$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(-3),$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $x=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(-3)=\\sqrt{-3+4}=\\sqrt{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(-1),$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}(-1)^{2}-(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}-(2)=6-2=4.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{33}{2}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302274 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right),\\,y=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right),\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | {{field-1}} | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{4}\\right)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(3),$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(3)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{6}\\right)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(1)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (4)}{6}\\right)=sin\\,\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302275 :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,y=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),\\,-1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(0),$ we substitute $t=0$ into $x=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(0)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (0)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into $y=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(1)=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{3}\\right)=tan\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $x(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $x=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(2)=cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi (2)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $-\\sqrt{3}$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302276 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t-7,\\,y=3-t,\\,t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nDetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $x=2t-7\\le 1$ for all $t\\le 4.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 1.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $y=3-t\\ge -1$ for all $t\\le 4.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302277 :question/problem "![](images/34/3497daefa76dd730942b7deb1f32c42a30823c9de953f45ec011218aff126b21.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $9,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $J(2,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=9.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302278 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}-4t,\\,y=t+1,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nDetermine the range of $x.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t+1)-2 \\\\  & =2(t-1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2(t-1)^{2}\\ge 0,$ we can conclude that\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302285 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $3-\\frac{1}{2}t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 2.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}-1\\ge 2$ for all $t\\ge 2.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302286 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4t-{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2}+3,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4t-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-4t) \\\\  & =-({t}^{2}-4t+4)+4 \\\\  & =-(t-2)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-(t-2)^{2}+4\\le 4,$ we conclude that the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that ${t}^{2}+3\\ge 3.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302287 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}-4t,\\,y=(t-1)^{2}+2,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{t}^{2}-4t \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =2({t}^{2}-2t+1)-2 \\\\  & =2(t-1)^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2(t-1)^{2}-2\\ge -2,$ we conclude that the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that $(t-1)^{2}+2\\ge 2.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302326 :question/problem "![](images/61/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,5).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(0,5),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302327 :question/problem "![](images/fc/fcb18bca21a0469f03093edc82cbdec7fbfd410acd434c88a4086876f02d35c6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,1)$ with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,1).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302337 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-5,\\,y=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,}+1,\\,t\\ge -4,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $t-5\\ge -9$ for all $t\\ge -4.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge -9.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\sqrt{t+4}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge -4.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302350 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3 \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{y+5}{2\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{28}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302351 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter  $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{10} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{10}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{11}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +12 \\\\ \\frac{y-12}{3\\sqrt{11}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y-12)^{2}}{99}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302352 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302353 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{9} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{81} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{48} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{48}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302354 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$  We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{72} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =11\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{11} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{11}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{121} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{72}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{121}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302355 :question/problem "![](images/31/31b10310b585d867ecf883db2a5d778ec5ec9160df03fc4487d2ef7fd0b9a3af.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/73/73cbae2c39fb2466fd59ced196c08ff2b6131b08a6a5dd4933e736404b12266e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302357 :question/problem "![](images/f2/f29c8db431035fda7e50771b3baaf6d19f51a60e26582957bbff0f70dbfdc5ed.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/24/24b56c8addea0d8a0f3effeeefc2788023c452e98b05d10db7893aeebde9ba5e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302358 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5bf161de62bc1b38aaeb87a327df551e8a7043f2867caa060d652cd065bece9.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6efc63426eae95430b0b5ddee44ab1523f69eb00ba26720374f7246712f7088a.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse in the **fourth quadrant** (where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\le 0$).\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\np=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302359 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f983fda4328227558e46aaaec454db2ccc0611331495c923ad1d90c7cb3df8a.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=8$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/10/101d9b26027bc5667009b0c7af69fa190c987c362122402fce62daec5833092f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302360 :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab9ef90cf863fee6776667a967d5939ba3564564d5d7626e07aaa868baea6a87.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,1),$ with $a=4$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we again interpret $\\theta $ as the central angle measured from the horizontal line through the center $(h,k).$ On the quarter-arc where both $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\ge 0$ (moving from the rightmost point to the topmost point), the parameter must satisfy $\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\n![](images/df/df6c12657a6a487f93ec3a6213be8ea16e68d363001bdf0ddb8ff68b5201a8c4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302362 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{4t}+1,\\,y=\\sqrt{t}+2,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{4t}+1\\ge 1$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\sqrt{t}+2\\ge 2$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302363 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{8-t},\\,y=\\sqrt{16-2t}+1,\\,t<8,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{8-t}>0$ for all $t<8.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx>0.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\sqrt{16-2t}+1>1$ for all $t<8.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny>1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302364 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}} $\\le $\n$x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-6\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 3 & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-3 \\\\ -3 & \\le -3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 3 \\\\ -3 & \\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le y\\le 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302365 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}{{field-5}}\n$y\\le $\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{9}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{9}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3} & \\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{3}\\le y\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302367 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\n \n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{5}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have \n$-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4} & \\le \\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{4}+2 & \\le \\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\le \\frac{3}{4}+2 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & \\le y\\le \\frac{11}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{5}{4}\\le y\\le \\frac{11}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302369 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4+1 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 4+1 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-3\\le x\\le 5.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{2} & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & \\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{7}{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{7}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302370 :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is \n{{field-4}}\n$\\le y$\n{{field-5}}{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3} & \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }{\\color{red}\\ge }-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\le -\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le \\frac{5}{3}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ \nwe have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{7}{3}\\le \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{29}{6} & \\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{29}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302392 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{f}(t)=2{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(1+5{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$, find $({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+5{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\cdot 2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+10t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(0)\\mathbf{j}+(10)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =12t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+10\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(12t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+10\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (2{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(1+5{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+(6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+10t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (6{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+10t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(12t\\cdot 2{t}^{3}+0\\cdot (-2t)+10\\cdot (1+5{t}^{2}))+((6{t}^{2})^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(10t)^{2}) \\\\  & =24{t}^{4}+0+10+50{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+4+100{t}^{2} \\\\  & =60{t}^{4}+150{t}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302424 :question/problem "![](images/52/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](images/1b/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302426 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{f}(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}-1⟩$, find $({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write: \n\n$$({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}0\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}6t⟩\\cdot ⟨{t}^{2}-1\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}^{3}-1⟩+⟨2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =(2\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1)+0\\cdot (2t)+6t\\cdot ({t}^{3}-1))+((2t)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-2+0+6{t}^{4}-6t+4{t}^{2}+4+9{t}^{4} \\\\  & =15{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302435 :question/problem "Given $\\mathbf{f}(t)=({t}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({t}^{2}-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$, evaluate $({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(t)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)$$\n\nLet's now find the first and second derivative of the function.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\cdot 2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}({\\mathbf{f}}^{'}\\cdot \\mathbf{f})^{'}(0) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{''}(0)\\cdot \\mathbf{f}(0)+{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(0)\\cdot {\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(0) \\\\  & =(6(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (({0}^{3}-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+({0}^{2}-2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+(3(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+8(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (3(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+8(0)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+8\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (-\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =0(-1)+2(-2)+(0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304789 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{g}(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}u \\\\ 5 \\\\ {u}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$, then  $(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}u \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}5\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}{u}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.167em}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u(t)} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u(t)\\end{bmatrix}{u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t\\operatorname{ln}t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}t}{t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304791 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{g}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{u}⟩$ and $u(t)={e}^{2t}$, then $(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}u,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}0,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\left(\\frac{1}{u}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{g}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{g}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{u(t)},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}(t)}⟩{u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}⟨\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{1}{({e}^{2t})^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{2t}},\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{4t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-2{e}^{-2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304891 :question/problem "Differentiate $2\\mathbf{f}(t)+3\\mathbf{g}(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{j}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =(5-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and the sum rules, we have \n\n$$\n(2\\mathbf{f}(t)+3\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}=2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}t\\mathbf{i}-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{f}(t)+3\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & =2{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+3{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+3(-\\operatorname{cos}t\\mathbf{i}-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-6\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+6\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}-3\\operatorname{cos}t\\mathbf{i}-6\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-6\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186305066 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{f}(u)={e}^{u+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{2u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{e}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $u(t)=2{t}^{2}$ then $(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)=\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u+1})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{2u})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\frac{1}{e}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{2u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{2t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})=4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\ (\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =({e}^{2{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2{e}^{4{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})4t \\\\  & =4t{e}^{2{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+8t{e}^{4{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
